				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tunedtoadeadchannel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304457); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=304457&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609579046" alt="tunedtoadeadchannel" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=304457)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/tunedtoadeadchannel" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(304457); return false;" >tunedtoadeadchannel</a></span> <span class="odate time_1320794707 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Nov 2011 23:25</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1301097">
									

<p>Indeed. The difference is, though, that while many writers attempting to convey that sort of characterization would slip into the trap of purple prose or over the top violence in their portrayal, you've kept things simple, clean, and clever. Some of the aphorisms in those sections made me happy to read just because of how darkly <em>perfect</em> they are for the mindset of the character you're portraying. Things like this:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That dear boy Boomer… not the sharpest razor in the apple, but a sweet lad all the same.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Everything about this line, from the oh so casual razor in apple metaphor to the implied almost loving patronization of a character implied (And later excellently demonstrated) to be a violent psychopath is phenominal.</p>

							</div>

								
					
